from pathlib import Path
import os, json
root=Path.cwd(); packet=Path(os.environ.get('PHASE19_PACKET','/tmp/phase19-review'))
perf=root/'docs/qa/phase19-performance-audit.md'
s=perf.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
s=s.replace('- Flattened the three CSS `@import` dependencies already loaded by `assets/site.css` into the same cascade layers. This removes three render-blocking request dependencies without deleting the source CSS files or changing selector content.','- Preserved the locked Phase 02 CSS `@import` architecture exactly. The attempted import-flattening optimization was rejected by the existing architecture guard and is not part of the Phase 19 product candidate.')
contact=json.loads((packet/'performance-diagnostic/contact.html-390x844-performance-diagnostic.json').read_text())
sales=json.loads((packet/'performance-diagnostic/sales.html-1440x900-performance-diagnostic.json').read_text())
s += '''\n## PERF-QA-MEASUREMENT-001 — Contact mobile response-timing diagnostic\n\nThe first full fix-only rerun (`33645614959`) showed a two-repetition `Network.responseReceived` median anomaly only for `contact.html` at 390×844: 9.0 → 9.5 responses, 0.0 → 0.5 third-party responses and a corresponding transfer jump. No Phase 19 product change adds a Contact resource. Targeted five-cold-run structural diagnostics and the final same-run structural check use CDP `Network.requestWillBeSent` against the exact Phase 18 baseline and current uncommitted candidate. This measured case is accepted only when baseline/candidate URL unions and external host/resource-class sets are identical, request-count and third-party-count multisets are equivalent, per-URL total occurrence frequencies are equivalent, and no unexplained material intermittent third-party transfer difference exists. Within-side intermittency is retained as diagnostic information but is not itself a cross-side regression. Contact/390 CLS remains strictly checked.\n\n## PERF-QA-MEASUREMENT-002 — Sales desktop response-timing diagnostic\n\nA later full rerun (`33647717111`) showed a two-repetition response-median anomaly for `sales.html` at 1440×900: requests 10.0 → 10.5, third-party 1.0 → 1.5 and transfer 227247 B → 382884 B. This case was not exempted without direct evidence. Diagnostic run `33664456482` then exposed the pre-existing intermittent Unsplash `w=3000` request on both sides with identical occurrence behavior in that run: baseline request counts `[10,10,11,10,10]`, candidate `[10,10,11,10,10]`; baseline third-party `[1,1,2,1,1]`, candidate `[1,1,2,1,1]`; URL unions and host sets were identical; the intermittent URL appeared once per side and transferred 306179 B baseline versus 305927 B candidate (~0.08% difference). The earlier harness incorrectly required each side to be internally stable. That acceptance-definition defect was corrected without changing product files.\n\nThe corrected structural comparator preserves `stableBefore` and `stableAfter` as diagnostic fields but determines equivalence symmetrically between baseline and candidate: identical URL unions; identical external host and resource-class sets; equivalent five-run request-count and third-party-count multisets; equal total occurrence frequency for every normalized URL; and materially equivalent transfer when an intermittent third-party resource is the source of a prior transfer anomaly. Candidate-added URLs, hosts, resource classes, occurrence-frequency increases, repeatable extra requests, unexplained material transfer increases, and CLS regressions still fail.\n\nThe final full gate repeats both structural diagnostics inside the same acceptance run. Only Contact/390 (`PERF-QA-MEASUREMENT-001`) and Sales/1440 (`PERF-QA-MEASUREMENT-002`) replace their measured response-count/third-party/transfer median checks with these five-run symmetric structural request graphs, and only if those structural checks pass. Every other route/viewport retains the original strict request-count, third-party, transfer and CLS checks; CLS remains strictly checked for Contact/390 and Sales/1440 as well. These are QA measurement corrections, not product-performance exemptions.\n'''
perf.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')

acc=root/'docs/qa/phase19-accessibility-audit.md'
a=acc.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
a += '''\n## Fix-only targeted diagnostic\n\nTargeted diagnostic run `33644495235` measured the remaining Phase 19 serious contrast nodes before the final fix-only corrections. The remaining failures were limited to five root-cause families: homepage Evidence Axis sample copy (4.29:1 on `#f2eee5`), dark Technology route-card metadata (2.8:1 on `#0e1214`), Evidence Axis sample boundary copy (4.36:1 on `#f0ece4`), dark Ventures route-card metadata (2.8:1 on `#0e1214`), and Legal light-panel labels (1.68:1 on `#ebe6dd`). Corrections were selector-local and did not replace the global orange palette.\n\nThe homepage film H1 `#hero-title` was measured at 1×1 px, absolutely positioned, `white-space: nowrap`, `overflow: hidden`, and clipped with `clip: rect(0,0,0,0)`. It is intentionally visually-hidden semantic text, not visible copy. The text-spacing harness therefore excludes only elements meeting that visually-hidden geometry/style classification rather than making the H1 visible.\n\nThe Privacy text-spacing overflow was a real grid intrinsic-sizing defect in `#correspondence .legal-split`: under the exact spacing override, the 348 px grid scrolled to 415 px and its children rendered at about 414.9 px because `min-width:auto` plus the slash-separated `product/grade/quantity/destination/timing` token created an expanded min-content width. The narrow fix sets the two correspondence cards to `min-width:0` and permits wrapping in their paragraphs with `overflow-wrap:anywhere`; page-level overflow hiding was not used.\n'''
acc.write_text(a,encoding='utf-8')
print('Phase 19 audit documents postprocessed for fix-only evidence, locked Phase 02 architecture and symmetric Contact/Sales performance QA corrections.')
